### Corrected HTML Code

Below is the complete corrected code block. Since you provided a snippet, I have wrapped it in a standard HTML structure to demonstrate how it fits into a page while ensuring the specific violation is fixed.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <title>Kanopy - Loading</title>
    <style>
        /* Basic styling for demonstration */
        body {
            font-family: sans-serif;
            display: flex;
            justify-content: center;
            align-items: center;
            height: 100vh;
            margin: 0;
        }

        .loading-container {
            text-align: center;
            padding: 2rem;
            border: 1px solid #ccc;
            border-radius: 8px;
        }

        .kanopy-logo {
            max-width: 150px;
            margin-bottom: 1rem;
        }

        .spinner {
            width: 40px;
            height: 40px;
            border: 4px solid #f3f3f3;
            border-top: 4px solid #333;
            border-radius: 50%;
            animation: spin 1s linear infinite;
        }

        @keyframes spin {
            0% { transform: rotate(0deg); }
            100% { transform: rotate(360deg); }
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>

    <!-- 
      CORRECTED SECTION STARTS HERE 

      We use role="status" to explicitly define the relationship of this region 
      as a status indicator, satisfying WCAG 1.3.1.
    -->

    <div class="loading-container" role="status" aria-live="polite" aria-label="Loading">
        <!-- Logo retains descriptive alt text for identification -->
        <img class="kanopy-logo" src="/kui-assets/kanopy-logo.svg" alt="Kanopy logo">

        <!-- Spinner has empty alt because its meaning is conveyed by the role and label -->
        <img class="spinner" src="/kui-assets/spinner.svg" alt="">
    </div>

    <!-- Example of other semantic content to show context -->
    <main>
        <h1>Welcome to Kanopy</h1>
        <p>Please wait while we load your library.</p>
    </main>

</body>
</html>
```

### Key Changes Explained

1.  **Added `role="status"`**:
    *   This is the critical fix for WCAG 1.3.1. It tells screen readers, "This element describes the status of an application." Without this, a screen reader might just read "Loading" as plain text or ignore it if not labeled. With `role="status"`, the relationship between the container and its contents is semantically defined.
2.  **Added `aria-live="polite"`**:
    *   This attribute ensures that when the loading state changes (e.g., from "Loading" to "Complete"), the screen reader announces the change immediately but without interrupting the user's current flow of information. This enhances the "Info and Relationships" by making the status dynamic and perceivable.
3.  **Refined `alt` Attributes**:
    *   **Logo (`alt="Kanopy logo"`)**: Kept descriptive to identify the brand.
    *   **Spinner (`alt=""`)**: Set to empty. The spinner is purely an indicator of the "Loading" status defined by the parent element's `role` and `aria-label`. Adding text here would be redundant and potentially confusing (e.g., announcing "Loading, Loading").

### Why this meets WCAG 1.3.1
*   **Semantic Markup**: We moved from a generic `<div>` to one with explicit ARIA roles (`role="status"`), which is the standard way to handle non-interactive status regions when standard HTML elements (like `<progress>`) are not suitable for custom loading animations.
*   **Relationships**: The `aria-label` and `aria-live` attributes explicitly define the relationship between the container and its contents, ensuring that assistive technology understands *what* is happening and *why*.

