### Analysis of WCAG 4.1.2 (Name, Role, Value) in this Context

The guideline **WCAG 4.1.2** mandates that markup must be used to facilitate accessibility by following HTML specifications and using ARIA appropriately when native HTML is insufficient.

In the context of your specific violation (`aria-tooltip-name`):
1.  **HTML Insufficiency:** The `<div>` elements with `role="tooltip"` are not native HTML elements. They do not have a natural name or role that conveys meaning to screen readers without ARIA support. Therefore, ARIA is *required* (not just "enhancing") to make them accessible.
2.  **The Violation:** The current code fails because it provides no name to the ARIA tooltip. Without a name, the element violates 4.1.2 because the markup (the `role="tooltip"`) implies a function (showing information) but fails to provide the *value* (the name of that information).
3.  **The Correction:** We must ensure every `role="tooltip"` has an explicit `aria-label` or `aria-labelledby` pointing to a named element. Furthermore, we should avoid using empty IDs (`id=""`) which are invalid in HTML and can cause parsing issues for assistive technologies.

### Corrected Complete Code

Below is the complete, corrected HTML structure. I have replaced the broken examples with functional, accessible implementations. I have also included a **Native HTML Tooltip** example as a best practice recommendation under 4.1.2, since native elements are preferred when possible.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <title>Accessible Tooltips Example</title>
    <style>
        /* Basic styling for demonstration */
        body { font-family: sans-serif; padding: 20px; }

        /* Tooltip Container Styling (Hidden by default) */
        .tooltip-container {
            position: relative;
            display: inline-block;
        }

        /* The Trigger Button/Link */
        .trigger-btn {
            cursor: pointer;
            padding: 10px;
            border: 1px solid #ccc;
            background-color: #f9f9f9;
        }

        /* The Tooltip Element Itself */
        [role="tooltip"] {
            visibility: hidden;
            opacity: 0;
            transition: opacity 0.3s, visibility 0.3s;
            position: absolute;
            z-index: 1000;
            background-color: #333;
            color: #fff;
            padding: 8px 12px;
            border-radius: 4px;
            font-size: 14px;
            max-width: 250px;
            text-align: center;
        }

        /* Show tooltip on hover (for mouse users) */
        .tooltip-container:hover [role="tooltip"],
        [role="tooltip"]:focus {
            visibility: visible;
            opacity: 1;
        }

        /* Focus indicator for keyboard users */
        [role="tooltip"]:focus {
            outline: 2px solid #005fcc;
            outline-offset: 2px;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>

    <h1>Accessible Tooltips (WCAG 4.1.2 Compliant)</h1>

    <!-- 
    CASE 1: Fixed Empty Label 
    We use aria-label to provide the name directly on the element.
    This satisfies 4.1.2 by ensuring the role has a value/name.
    -->
    <div class="tooltip-container" id="container-1">
        <button class="trigger-btn" aria-describedby="tooltip-1-text">
            🚫 Icon (No Delete)
        </button>
        <!-- The tooltip element itself does not need content, just the label -->
        <div role="tooltip" id="tooltip-1" aria-label="This action will permanently delete your account. Please be sure."></div>
    </div>

    <!-- 
    CASE 2: Fixed Empty String / Missing Reference 
    We use aria-labelledby pointing to a separate element that has its own accessible name.
    This is useful for dynamic content where the text might change without re-rendering the tooltip div.
    -->
    <div class="tooltip-container" id="container-2">
        <button class="trigger-btn" aria-describedby="tooltip-2-text">
            ⚠️ Icon (Warning)
        </button>

        <!-- The referenced element must have a natural name or its own aria-label -->
        <span id="tooltip-2-text" style="display:none;">
            Warning: You are about to lose unsaved changes.
        </span>

        <div role="tooltip" id="tooltip-2" aria-labelledby="tooltip-2-text"></div>
    </div>

    <!-- 
    CASE 3: Best Practice - Native HTML Tooltip 
    WCAG 4.1.2 prefers native elements when they exist. 
    <details> and <summary> are natively accessible and do not require ARIA attributes 
    for basic functionality, reducing complexity and potential errors.
    -->
    <div class="tooltip-container" id="container-3">
        <button class="trigger-btn" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="details-3-content">
            ℹ️ Icon (Info)
        </button>

        <!-- Native details element acts as the tooltip container -->
        <details id="details-3-content">
            <summary>Hover or click for more info</summary>
            <p>This is the native accessible content. It works with screen readers 
               without needing complex ARIA manipulation.</p>
        </details>
    </div>

    <!-- 
    CASE 4: Fixed Empty Referenced Element 
    The referenced element (#emptydiv) now has a natural name (text content).
    -->
    <div class="tooltip-container" id="container-4">
        <button class="trigger-btn" aria-describedby="tooltip-4-text">
            📝 Icon (Details)
        </button>

        <!-- The target element has natural text content, making it accessible -->
        <span id="tooltip-4-text">Specific technical details regarding this feature.</span>

        <div role="tooltip" id="tooltip-4" aria-labelledby="tooltip-4-text"></div>
    </div>

</body>
</html>
```

### Explanation of Corrections against WCAG 4.1.2

1.  **Eliminated Empty/Invalid IDs:** Removed `id=""` attributes which are invalid HTML and can confuse parsers. All IDs now contain valid, descriptive strings (e.g., `tooltip-1`, `container-1`).
2.  **Provided Accessible Names:**
    *   In **Case 1**, we used `aria-label` directly on the tooltip. This is the most robust method for static tooltips.
